{"chapter": "sets", "node_kind": "section_title", "parent_tag": "000A", "placeholders": {}, "render": {"prefix": "\\section{", "suffix": "}\n\\label{sets-section-classes}\n\n"}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:859f930c44df812b5df0ca8663b5a14087c6d3fabf47b29eef9cf9a556a018c8", "source_text": "Classes", "source_text_hash": "sha256:84637286c5b731d749ff9574416ae2de473f72c85aca744fcd192a35c70913ca", "unit_id": "tag:000A:title"}
{"chapter": "sets", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "000A", "placeholders": {"EMPHCLOSE_0001": "}", "EMPHCLOSE_0002": "}", "EMPHCLOSE_0003": "}", "EMPHOPEN_0001": "\\emph{", "EMPHOPEN_0002": "\\emph{", "EMPHOPEN_0003": "\\emph{", "MATH_0001": "$\\phi(x, p_1, \\ldots, p_n)$", "MATH_0002": "$$\nC = \\{x : \\phi(x, p_1, \\ldots, p_n)\\}\n$$", "MATH_0003": "$R$", "MATH_0004": "$R$", "MATH_0005": "$M$", "MATH_0006": "$R$"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\noindent\n", "suffix": "\n\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:64117d13f6c784f395b36121cfcb3dd4393f0a59ce47810b3f81740c4c3911f1", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:1a15bf0567bec29116a94726fede702b181854c654b5e30cb28df753f189ab90", "source_text": "Informally we use the notion of a <EMPHOPEN_0001>class<EMPHCLOSE_0001>. Given a formula <MATH_0001>, we call <MATH_0002> a <EMPHOPEN_0002>class<EMPHCLOSE_0002>. A class is easier to manipulate than the formula that defines it, but it is not strictly speaking a mathematical object. For example, if <MATH_0003> is a ring, then we may consider the class of all <MATH_0004>-modules (since after all we may translate the sentence “<MATH_0005> is an <MATH_0006>-module” into a formula in set theory, which then defines a class). A <EMPHOPEN_0003>proper class<EMPHCLOSE_0003> is a class which is not a set.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:d08b21c50129f87490f35c67aa48d771f8b8dce42cd06a4de190c0fab1a04335", "unit_id": "tag:000A:p001"}
{"chapter": "sets", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "000A", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$R$"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\medskip\\noindent\n", "suffix": "\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:e5fc7892ed5d3c89859f320312560ac7543d92ccd56c8b74f4e03c4acf0d532c", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:cea8cebdd09466e33f4ca2aedfca0e4658e6481686fb75d81a583eaaa2722fd6", "source_text": "In this way we may consider the category of <MATH_0001>-modules, which is a “big” category—in other words, it has a proper class of objects. Similarly, we may consider the “big” category of schemes, the “big” category of rings, etc.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:dae3c113ea981b22f1002efbbc24418ceb919278b238d3a0ca5f39d97a7af59c", "unit_id": "tag:000A:p002"}
